// Naive schedule: single worker, no decomposition.
//
// Landed TASK-0453.05 (rigour epic P5). The CAP-HIT sibling of
// 21-jacobi-converge/schedules/naive.sched.nuc. Every kernel runs on
// `host`; no cross-worker dataflow means no `transfer` directives. The
// result must be bit-identical to reference.bin (the last computed
// generation `field[ITERS_CAP]` on cap-hit).
//
// HOST-ONLY (single-worker) schedule, but RUNS ON ALL 7 TIER-1 BACKENDS
// (epic S7, TASK-0341.02.01.08): `workers = { host }` means
// `used_workers <= 1`, so every backend delegates to the SHARED
// `render_single_worker_main`. The multi-worker `break_cond` fail-loud
// guard is never reached (it fires only for `partition=workers`). This is
// the WORST-CASE full-cap replay witness: the `until` predicate never
// fires inside the cap, so every backend runs the full `0..ITERS_CAP+1`.
// A distributed (collective-break) convergence schedule remains out of
// reach — the remaining scope of epic S7.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_input       on host;
    place save_output      on host;
    place jacobi5_or_seed  on host;
    place max_abs_acc      on host;
    place ident            on host;

    // No loop transforms.
    // No transfer directives — there are no cross-worker edges.
}
